{
  "dom_id": "step-q27762",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192941/assets/0d3b179cb8f464ca794d7529414af8421a327011b53418d08578bbf0dd7469d2.png)\n\nOn which interval is the function $y=f(x)$, shown above, decreasing?",
  "worked_solution": "If we follow the graph from left to right, we can see that: \n\n- For $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (-3,-2)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in (-2,-1)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (-1,0)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in (0,1)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (1,2)$ the graph rises\n\n- For $x\\in (2,3)$ the graph falls\n\n- For $x\\in (3,\\infty )$ the graph rises\n\nThe function is decreasing on the intervals where it never rises: $(-\\infty ,-3),$  $(-2,-1),$  $(0,1),$ and $(2,3).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
